# Original-record counts with other formation events allowed

A supplied one-particle wave preparation changes a local original-record
coincidence count even when other formation events are permitted before,
between and after the selected events. This is an unrestricted count, with
its own variance, rather than the earlier first-two-event probability.
The controlled asymptotic still requires decreasing rates and shrinking
lag windows. On a fixed graph the sufficient rates also make global births
rare; removing the conditioning does not establish a signal in a regime
with appreciable background activity.

Section A proves a local disturbance estimate uniform in total volume and
electric cutoff. Section B extends its commutator proof to two insertions,
then combines it with the prepared full-Hamiltonian lag estimate. Section C
attributes stronger fixed-graph and statistical consequences to the blind
unrestricted-count PRE. The prepared-wave constants are not uniform in volume.

Use a fixed finite simple cubic torus with each period even and at least six,
all A sites occupied with plus charge and all B sites initially empty, the
parent Gauss constraint and the normalized vacuum/one-particle packets of the
weak-field theorem. The initial zero-winding quotient is a preparation
restriction, never a restriction on postbirth matter or field states.
The exact selected resolved marks and plaquette are those in the original
readout parent: a=(0,0,0), b=(-1,0,0), d=(1,1,0), f=(2,1,0), with
intermediate c=(1,0,0), e=(0,1,0). Either specified sign at each birth is fixed.
Their initial effect is 23I+W_p+W_p*, and each initial single-mark effect is 5I.
Neither identity is promoted to arbitrary later matter sectors.

The full supplied compensated matter-field law and original instrument remain
unchanged. The parents are the
[common limit](LOCAL_COMPENSATION_COMMON_FIELD_RECORD_LIMIT_BOUNDED_THEOREM_NOTE_2026-09-24.md),
